+# Bug Assessment: Preserve orders after customer deletion and fix order pagination
+
+- **Slug**: ticket
+- **Created**: 2026-11-18T00:00:00+00:00
+- **Source**: pasted text
+- **Verdict**: valid
+- **Severity**: high
+
+## Report (verbatim or summarized)
+
+Support reports that deleting a customer makes the admin order detail and customer-order endpoints return 404, although orders must remain available with their captured billing, delivery, and email data. The customer-order endpoint must also work for deleted customer ids, re-registration with the same email must not inherit old orders, and the admin order list must return later pages with correct totals.
+
+## Symptom
+
+The customer-order API rejects an id when the customer row no longer exists, and the order detail path depends on query/facade behavior that is not resilient to a missing customer. The admin list's pagination/empty-list handling loses the query totals and does not reliably return requested pages; expected behavior is stable order retrieval by the denormalized customer id and email plus zero-based, newest-first pages with totals.
+
+## Reproduction
+
+1. Create a customer, place one or more orders, then delete the customer via `DELETE /api/v1/private/customer/{id}`.
+2. Request `GET /api/v1/private/orders/{orderId}` and `GET /api/v1/private/orders/customers/{customerId}`; both should remain usable after deletion.
+3. Register again with the deleted email and verify the new customer cannot see the old orders.
+4. Request `GET /api/v1/private/orders?count={n}&page={p}` for page 0 and later pages, with and without status/email/name/id filters, and compare returned rows, `recordsTotal`, and `totalPages`.
+
+## Suspected Code Paths
+
+- `sm-shop/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/shop/store/api/v1/order/OrderApi.java:112-155` — customer-order endpoint first loads the `Customer` entity and sends 404 before querying orders.
+- `sm-shop/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/shop/store/controller/order/facade/OrderFacadeImpl.java:936-966` — list conversion returns an empty response with `recordsTotal=0` when the current page is empty, discarding the repository count and total pages.
+- `sm-core/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/core/business/repositories/order/OrderRepositoryImpl.java:138-260` — order filtering/counting and pagination are implemented here; the count-zero early return and page calculation are not preserved consistently, and the fetch-join query is paginated directly.
+- `sm-core/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/core/business/utils/RepositoryHelper.java:22-42` — zero-based page offsets and `totalPages` calculation are centralized here and currently use an off-by-one formula.
+- `sm-core/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/core/business/repositories/order/OrderRepository.java:8-20` — order detail uses mandatory fetch joins for associated order collections, making detail retrieval fragile when optional historical associations are absent.
+- `sm-shop/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/shop/store/controller/order/facade/OrderFacadeImpl.java:1110-1155` — detail lookup deliberately tolerates a missing customer, but must continue populating the order from its stored snapshot fields.
+- `sm-core-model/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/core/model/order/Order.java:68-73` — orders retain `CUSTOMER_ID` as a scalar and therefore can be queried independently of the customer row.
+
+## Root Cause Hypothesis
+
+Confidence: high. The customer-order controller incorrectly treats customer existence as a prerequisite even though orders store a scalar customer id. The list path also conflates an empty requested page with zero matching records, while `RepositoryHelper` calculates pages using `(count / size) + 1`; these behaviors explain missing later-page rows/totals. The detail repository's mandatory collection fetches are an additional fragility for historical orders and should be made optional or split into a root-order lookup followed by safe association loading.
+
+## Proposed Remediation
+
+**Preferred**: Query customer orders directly by `Order.customerId` and merchant, without loading the customer entity. Return an empty `ReadableOrderList` carrying the repository count and page totals when there are no rows, and populate each returned order's customer only when the customer still exists. Keep order identity separate from email so a newly registered account with the same email has a new id and cannot inherit old orders. Make admin list pagination use a count query plus a correctly bounded zero-based page, calculate `ceil(total/count)`, and preserve totals on out-of-range pages. Make order detail retrieval resilient to missing optional order associations while retaining billing, delivery, email, status, history, and payment data.
+
+**Alternatives** (optional):
+- Replace the collection-fetch query with two-phase id pagination and association loading; this is more robust for large orders but is a broader repository change.
+- Add a database foreign-key `ON DELETE SET NULL`; this would lose the customer id needed for historical lookup and is not suitable for the stated API contract.
+
+**Files likely to change**:
+- `sm-shop/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/shop/store/api/v1/order/OrderApi.java`
+- `sm-shop/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/shop/store/controller/order/facade/OrderFacadeImpl.java`
+- `sm-core/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/core/business/repositories/order/OrderRepository.java`
+- `sm-core/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/core/business/repositories/order/OrderRepositoryImpl.java`
+- `sm-core/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/core/business/utils/RepositoryHelper.java`
+- `sm-shop/src/test/java/com/salesmanager/test/shop/integration/order/OrderApiIntegrationTest.java`
+
+**Tests to add or update**:
+- Verify deleted customers' orders remain retrievable by order id and customer id, including captured order contact/address fields and null customer.
+- Verify a new account using the old email has no access to the old account's orders.
+- Verify filtered admin pages are newest-first, later pages contain rows, out-of-range pages are empty with unchanged totals, and empty customer ids report zero totals.
+
+## Risks & Considerations
+
+- Historical order retrieval must not expose a newly registered customer merely because the email matches; customer identity must remain the stored id.
+- Fetch-join pagination can duplicate rows or be applied in memory; tests should cover multiple products/attributes per order.
+- Existing clients may rely on `totalPages` legacy semantics, so the endpoint contract and zero-based page behavior should be tested explicitly.
+- Changes should preserve order status, history, and payment transaction APIs.
+
+## Open Questions
+
+- None blocking automated remediation.
